      "title": "Calculating a Directional Derivative"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=2 \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} \\right)}$ at the point $(0,-3)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=3\\,\\mathbf{i}+1\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}(3\\,\\mathbf{i}+1\\,\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} \\right)}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} \\right)})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4 x}{x^{2} + y^{2}},\\frac{4 y}{x^{2} + y^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(0,-3) & =⟨\\frac{4 (0)}{(0)^{2} + (-3)^{2}},\\frac{4 (-3)}{(0)^{2} + (-3)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,- \\frac{4}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(0,-3) & =∇f(0,-3)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨0,- \\frac{4}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10},\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot (3)+\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\cdot (1)}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
