    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=3 x + 4 y^{2}$ at the point $(-2,3)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=-1\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-1\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x + 4 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x + 4 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,8 y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-2,3) & =⟨3,8 (3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(-2,3) & =∇f(-2,3)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨3,24⟩\\cdot ⟨- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5},\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)\\cdot (-1)+(24)\\cdot (2)}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =9 \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
