    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} \\right)}$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=-4\\,\\mathbf{i}-5\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-5)^{2}}=\\sqrt{41} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{41}}(-4\\,\\mathbf{i}-5\\,\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} \\right)}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} \\right)})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2 x}{x^{2} + y^{2}},\\frac{2 y}{x^{2} + y^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =⟨\\frac{2 (2)}{(2)^{2} + (-1)^{2}},\\frac{2 (-1)}{(2)^{2} + (-1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},- \\frac{2}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{′}(2,-1) & =∇f(2,-1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},- \\frac{2}{5}⟩\\cdot ⟨- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{41}}{41},- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{41}}{41}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\cdot (-4)+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot (-5)}{\\sqrt{41}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{41}}{205}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
